@startuml
title How a hardware RNG call became a software PRNG call
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #F3EEFB
  BorderColor #7A5BB5
  DiamondBackgroundColor #FCFAFF
  DiamondBorderColor #7A5BB5
}
skinparam ArrowColor #7A5BB5

start
:board sets MICROPY_HW_ENABLE_RNG = 0\n"this code replaces normal RNG module";
note right
  the intent was to disable upstream's
  RNG module in favour of the board's
  own, more paranoid, version
end note

:upstream ports/stm32/rng.c compiles\nthe #else branch: the yasmarang PRNG;
:that object defines a global rng_get();

:board file COLDCARD_MK4/rng.c defines\nrandom_buffer(), random32(), pyb_rng_get\nbut NOT rng_get();

:libngu is compiled with\nCHIP_TRNG_32() defined as rng_get();

:linker resolves rng_get\nto the only definition available;<<#FDEEEE>>

:ngu.random.bytes() XORs two\nsoftware PRNGs together;<<#FDEEEE>>

:seed.py hashes the result with SHA256d\n"to mitigate any possible bias";
note right
  hashing whitens the output: it looks
  random to every statistical test while
  carrying the same low entropy
end note

:generated seed has far fewer than\n128 bits of unpredictability;<<#FDEEEE>>

stop
@enduml
